    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d77">

                <pb facs="0267" n="216" xml:id="pg216"/>

                <head>77. Memorandum From Robert Pastor of
                    the National Security Council Staff to the President’s Assistant for National
                    Security Affairs (Brzezinski)<anchor
                        n="1" xml:id="d77-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1978-07-17">July 17, 1978</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <list subtype="subject" type="description">
                    <label>SUBJECT</label>
                    <item>Letter to Nicaraguan President Somoza</item>
                </list>
                <p>Last week, our Ambassador orally conveyed the President’s letter to General
                        Somoza of Nicaragua.<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d77-f2"/> Since then, there has been considerable violence in
                    Nicaragua, amounting to at least seven deaths. On July 19, a broad front is
                    planning a general strike, and there appears to be increasing polarization in
                    the country.</p>
                <p>Our Ambassador fears that if he delivers the letter, that Somoza may use it to strengthen his position.
                    He has therefore recommended that he not deliver the letter when it arrives by
                        pouch.<anchor n="3" xml:id="d77-f3"/>
                    Vaky strongly supports the
                    recommendation of the Ambassador; he believes that there is a qualitative
                    difference between an oral message and the letter, and the latter is certainly
                    easier to use for Somoza’s purposes.
                        Christopher is less certain whether
                    this justifies the President’s attention.</p>
                <p>I think there are three options:</p>
                <p>1. Not deliver the letter (Ambassador and Vaky recommendation).<anchor n="4" xml:id="d77-f4"/></p>
                <p>2. Deliver the letter, but<anchor n="5" xml:id="d77-f5"/> stress the President’s
                    concern with recent developments in Nicaragua since the letter was originally
                        sent.<anchor n="6" xml:id="d77-f6"/></p>
                <p>3. Deliver the letter.</p>
                <p rendition="#section-heading #italic">RECOMMENDATION</p>
                <p>I would favor Option 1 or 2.</p>
                <p>Shall I draft a memo from you to the President?<anchor n="7" xml:id="d77-f7"
                    /></p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d77-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d77-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Carter Library, National Security Affairs, Staff Material, North/South,
                    Pastor Files, Country Files, Box 33, Nicaragua: 5–7/78. Confidential.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d77-f2" n="2" xml:id="d77-f2-content">
                <p>See <ref target="#d76">Document 76</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d77-f3" n="3" xml:id="d77-f3-content">
                <p>See <ref target="#d76-f5">footnote 5, Document 76</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d77-f4" n="4" xml:id="d77-f4-content">
                <p>In the left-hand margin next to the recommendation, Aaron wrote: “My recommendation P[resident] will look foolish.
                        DA.”</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d77-f5" n="5" xml:id="d77-f5-content">
                <p>Inderfurth crossed out the word “but”
                    and wrote the word “and.” He also wrote in the left-hand margin next to the
                    recommendation: “My suggestion. RI.”</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d77-f6" n="6" xml:id="d77-f6-content">
                <p>Brzezinski checked the approval
                    option.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d77-f7" n="7" xml:id="d77-f7-content">
                <p>Brzezinski did not indicate his approval
                    or disapproval of the recommendation.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
